From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: the surface machinery and the built-in framebuffer

Two drivers, one patch, because they cannot be separated: `AppleCLCD` publishes
the panel memory *as* a `SweetSurface` (`sweet_surface_create_physical`), looks
swaps up by surface id (`sweet_surface_copy`), and hands the default surface's
port to the user client (`sweet_surface_port_in_task`). A framebuffer without
the surface table links against nothing and matches nothing.

`IOCoreSurfaceRoot` is the other half of the same story. iOS userland does not
talk to surfaces directly - it opens `IOSurfaceRoot` and creates them through
it - so the framebuffer's own surface is useless to CoreAnimation unless the
root user client is registered too. Both are `IOResources` personalities, which
is what makes them cheap: no provider, no DT node, no board values.

**What is actually board-specific here: nothing.** `SweetSurface.cpp` contains
zero board macros (the `SWEET_SURFACE_*` names are its own limits, not hardware),
and `SweetFramebuffer.cpp` reads its geometry from `PE_current_console()`, which
on both boards is `PE_state.video` copied out of `boot_args->Video` by
`pexpert/arm/pe_init.c:406-417`. On milanf that is 1080x2460 at 0x85200000 with
stride 4320, straight out of `shim/milanf-config.h` and the shim's
`ba->Video.v_*` assignments. So this is a gate widen plus one genuine logic fix.

**The logic fix is the interesting part.** The presentation size is not free:
the iOS userland only carries status-bar and layout data for real iPhone
screens, so both boards present as the iPhone X panel - 1125x2436, 375x812
points at 3x - and the physical panel is centered inside that canvas at 1:1.
The gate for that was

    if (!native && fWidth <= SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_W && fHeight <= SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_H)

which is correct for exactly one panel shape: one that *fits inside* the canvas.
Sweet's 1080x2400 does (offsets +22, +18: the canvas is cropped at the edges by
the panel). Milanf's 1080x2460 does not - it is 24 rows *taller* than the canvas
- so the condition fails, `fLogical*` stay at the physical size, and the service
would report `GetDisplaySize` 1080x2460 while its provider `disp0` publishes
`canvas-width 1125 / canvas-height 2436` from the ADT. Two different answers to
the same question, and the userland believes both.

The fix is to drop the size test and let the offsets go negative, because the
copy already handles that direction correctly:

    dx -= fOffX;
    dy -= fOffY;
    if (dy < 0) { sy -= dy; h += dy; dy = 0; }      /* clips the top edge */

with `fOffY = (2436 - 2460) / 2 = -12`, `dy` becomes `+12` for a full-canvas
layer, the height is clamped to `fHeight - dy = 2448`, and the whole 2436-row
canvas lands on panel rows 12..2447 at 1:1. Nothing is scaled and nothing is
cropped vertically - the canvas is *letterboxed*, 12 rows above and 12 below.
Horizontal behaviour is unchanged: `fOffX = (1125 - 1080) / 2 = +22`, so the
canvas is cropped by 22 px left and 23 px right, exactly as on sweet.

The letterbox rows are the one thing the swap path never touches, so they would
keep whatever the boot console last drew there forever - white text on black
across the top and bottom edge of the screen, which reads as corruption. They
are zeroed once in `start()` when the offsets are negative (24 rows on milanf,
no-op on sweet, whose offsets are both positive). Deliberately *not* a full
panel clear: the kernel console stays visible until the first swap, which is the
whole point of the early framebuffer in 0086.

Finally the personalities. The milanf arm of `iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp` gains
`IOCoreSurfaceRoot` (IOResources) and `AppleCLCD` (`IOPlatformDevice`, matching
`'sweet,display'` - the name `tools/mkadt-milanf.py:231` already publishes on
`/arm-io/disp0`). Guard-widen and personality ship together for the reason noted
in 0088: a personality whose class does not exist only earns an IOKit
"can't find class" line, and a class with no personality is dead code.

The derived size needs no new constant: 1080 x 2460 x 4 = 10,627,200 bytes, and
the carveout the shim points at is `cont_splash_region` - 12 MB at 0x85200000.
It fits with 1.6 MB to spare, which is also why `BOARD_EARLY_FB_*` in 0086 could
use the same 32 MB L2 block for the console.

Verified without a compiler: fresh ci-art3, 0086-0097 applied one at a time in
order, every one exit 0, every touched file CR=0, patch CR=0. Link unverified;
CI in the morning.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
index a62416c..5d67e9f 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@
 /*
- * Built-in IOMobileFramebuffer for SWEET: the ABL's continuous-splash
- * framebuffer (1080x2400 a8r8g8b8 in RAM, the panel scans it out by
- * itself) as the main display, driven by CoreAnimation's CPU renderer.
+ * Built-in IOMobileFramebuffer for the Qualcomm boards: the ABL's
+ * continuous-splash framebuffer (a8r8g8b8 in RAM, the panel scans it
+ * out by itself) as the main display, driven by CoreAnimation's CPU
+ * renderer. Geometry comes from the boot_args video block, so the two
+ * boards differ only in what the shim put there.
  *
  * IOMobileFramebuffer.framework (iOS 16.7) counts /arm-io children with
  * device_type "display-subsystem", waits for that many services matching
@@ -38,7 +40,7 @@
 
 extern "C" int sweet_console_muted;     /* osfmk/console/serial_console.c */
 
-#if defined(SWEET)
+#if defined(SWEET) || defined(MILANF)
 #include <IOKit/IOService.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOUserClient.h>
 #include <IOKit/IOKitKeys.h>
@@ -74,8 +76,10 @@ enum {
  * The iOS userland only has status bar / layout data for real iPhone
  * screens (UIKit: "No status bar visual provider for screen", the red
  * rdar://45025538 bar for 360x800 points). The display is therefore
- * presented as the iPhone X panel, 1125x2436 (375x812 points @3x), and the
- * centered 1080x2400 part of it is shown on the 1080x2400 panel.
+ * presented as the iPhone X panel, 1125x2436 (375x812 points @3x), and
+ * the physical panel is centered inside it at 1:1 - cropped where the
+ * panel is smaller than the canvas (sweet, 1080x2400), letterboxed
+ * where it is larger (milanf, 1080x2460).
  * Boot-arg sweetfb_native=1 presents the real panel size instead.
  */
 #define SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_W              1125
@@ -170,7 +174,13 @@ AppleCLCD::start(IOService *provider)
 	PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_native", &native, sizeof(native));
 	fLogicalW = fWidth;
 	fLogicalH = fHeight;
-	if (!native && fWidth <= SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_W && fHeight <= SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_H) {
+	/*
+	 * No size test: a panel smaller than the canvas is cropped by a
+	 * positive offset and a taller one is letterboxed by a negative
+	 * one. The swap path clamps both directions (see the dy < 0 case),
+	 * so the canvas is always 1:1 and never scaled.
+	 */
+	if (!native) {
 		fLogicalW = SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_W;
 		fLogicalH = SWEET_FB_LOGICAL_H;
 	}
@@ -187,6 +197,34 @@ AppleCLCD::start(IOService *provider)
 		OSSafeReleaseNULL(fWorkLoop);
 		return false;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * A panel taller than the canvas leaves rows no swap will ever
+	 * reach. Black them once so they do not keep the boot console
+	 * forever: 12 rows above and 12 below on milanf, none on sweet.
+	 * Not a full clear - the console stays visible until first swap.
+	 */
+	if (fOffY < 0) {
+		/* Rows above and below the canvas no swap ever reaches. */
+		uint32_t above = (uint32_t)(-(int64_t)fOffY);
+		uint32_t seen  = fLogicalH;
+
+		if (above > fHeight) {
+			above = fHeight;
+		}
+		if (seen > fHeight - above) {
+			seen = fHeight - above;
+		}
+		if (above > 0) {
+			memset((void *)fPixels, 0, (size_t)above * fRowBytes);
+		}
+		if (above + seen < fHeight) {
+			uint32_t below = fHeight - above - seen;
+			uint8_t *tail  = (uint8_t *)fPixels +
+			    (uint64_t)(above + seen) * fRowBytes;
+
+			memset(tail, 0, (size_t)below * fRowBytes);
+		}
+	}
 	fVsync = IOTimerEventSource::timerEventSource(this, &AppleCLCD::vsyncFired);
 	if (fVsync == NULL || fWorkLoop->addEventSource(fVsync) != kIOReturnSuccess) {
 		IOLog("SWEET FB: no vsync timer\n");
@@ -568,4 +606,4 @@ IOMobileFramebufferUserClient::externalMethod(uint32_t selector, IOExternalMetho
 	}
 	return ret;
 }
-#endif /* SWEET */
+#endif /* SWEET || MILANF */
